<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ language }}" dir="{{ direction }}">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title>{% block title %}{{ copy.product }}{% endblock %}</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ assets }}/shell.css">
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ assets }}/shell-components.css">
</head>
<body data-language="{{ language }}">
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">{{ copy.skip }}</a>
  {#
    The persistent frame. It degrades by surface rather than rendering one fixed row, because
    `_unavailable` and `_no_membership` are handed nothing but a language -- "takes no cause, so it
    can disclose none" -- and `StrictUndefined` would turn an unconditional `organization_name` into
    a 500 on the two surfaces that exist to absorb failure.

    So the brand and the language control, which need only `language`, are on every surface, and the
    organization and `Team` appear where a resolved organization was passed. That is `FR-050` holding
    by construction rather than by care: `unavailable` never receives the context, so its frame is
    the same whichever cause brought the reader there.

    `Team` is the only destination. `FR-049` forbids rendering an entry for a surface with no
    implementation, and the dispatcher answers exactly two.
  #}
  <header class="shell-identity">
    {#
      Named by both spans in source order, the way the organization control is, so the accessible
      name is "KHEPRI home page" and begins with the text a reader can see. An `aria-label` here
      *replaced* that text: on the Arabic shell the accessible name held no `KHEPRI` at all, so a
      speech-input reader who said the one word on the control could not operate it. The wordmark
      still needs the words -- a picture of a name is not a name -- so they follow it.
    #}
    <a class="brand"
       href="{{ prefix }}/{{ language }}"
       aria-labelledby="frame-brand-name frame-brand-purpose">
      <span id="frame-brand-name">KHEPRI</span>
      <span id="frame-brand-purpose" class="visually-hidden">{{ copy.frame_home_label }}</span>
    </a>
    {% if organization_id is defined and organization_id %}
    {#
      Named by both spans in source order, so the accessible name is "Acme Trading Change
      organization" rather than a bare organization name whose purpose a screen reader cannot infer.
      `dir="auto"` on the name itself: `FR-055` requires a Latin run inside Arabic prose to carry
      its direction, and an organization name is the most likely Latin run on this surface -- but
      `Organization.create` restricts the name to no script, so it is just as likely to be Arabic.
      `auto` derives the direction from the value's first strong character, which is `ltr` for the
      Latin run `FR-055` is about and `rtl` for an Arabic name that a fixed `ltr` would have
      reordered around its own punctuation and digits. Either way the run is isolated, which is
      what the requirement asks for; the fixed value only happened to be right for half the inputs.
    #}
    {% if organization_name %}
    <a class="frame-organization"
       href="{{ prefix }}/{{ language }}"
       aria-labelledby="frame-organization-name frame-organization-action">
      <span id="frame-organization-name" dir="auto">{{ organization_name }}</span>
      <span id="frame-organization-action" class="visually-hidden">{{ copy.frame_organization_label }}</span>
    </a>
    {% else %}
    {#
      A resolved organization with no display name still has surfaces. The control above is *named*
      by its name span, so with nothing to put there it would be a link whose accessible name is the
      purpose alone -- "Change organization" attached to no organization. The chooser it leads to is
      reachable from the wordmark, so the frame drops the control and keeps the navigation: an empty
      name is a missing label, not a missing organization, and `_active_organization_name` returns
      the same empty string for both a blank name and a listing that carries none.
    #}
    <a class="frame-organization-fallback"
       href="{{ prefix }}/{{ language }}">{{ copy.frame_organization_label }}</a>
    {% endif %}
    <nav class="frame-surfaces" aria-label="{{ copy.team_title }}">
      <a href="{{ prefix }}/{{ language }}/{{ organization_id }}/team">{{ copy.team_title }}</a>
    </nav>
    {% endif %}
    {#
      `FR-047` makes the language a property of the address, so this swaps the segment and keeps the
      surface -- scenario 11's "language switch mid-surface, position preserved". `lang` is set so a
      screen reader pronounces the target language in that language rather than in the page's.

      `language_switch` is the one surface-shaped exception, and it exists because the control
      cannot keep its promise on `invitation_issued`. That surface is a `POST` result with no
      address of its own, so there is no address to re-request in the other language: every
      destination the control could name is a *different* surface, and reaching it destroys the
      only plaintext copy of the invitation token, which `issue` returns once and never again.
      A control that says "switch language" and means "discard the secret you were told to copy"
      is worse than no control, so that surface renders none. `FR-054`'s parity is unharmed --
      the surface renders in both languages with the same actions in each -- and `FR-055` asks a
      switch to preserve position, which is a constraint on a switch that exists.
    #}
    {% if language_switch %}
    {#
      `dir` goes on the text and not on the anchor. `.frame-language` is a flex item carrying
      `margin-inline-start: auto` to hold the inline end, and a logical property resolves against
      the *element's own* direction -- so `dir` here, on a label whose script is always the
      opposite of the page's, resolved that margin to the wrong physical side on both shells and
      the control stopped sitting at the end. The isolation `FR-055` asks for belongs to the run;
      the layout belongs to the page. Splitting them gives each the direction it needs.
    #}
    <a class="frame-language"
       href="{{ prefix }}/{{ alternate }}{{ surface_path }}"
       lang="{{ copy.frame_language_code }}"><span dir="auto">{{ copy.frame_language }}</span></a>
    {% endif %}
  </header>
  <main id="main-content" class="shell-main" tabindex="-1">
    {% block content %}{% endblock %}
  </main>
</body>
</html>
